# Rotation for the installer's own transcript, /var/log/maran/install.log.
#
# Installed to /etc/logrotate.d/maran-install, root:root 0644, by installer/lib/41-log-retention.sh
# (issue #131).
#
# It was the last file under /var/log/maran that nothing bounded. The other three policies cover the
# panel vhost's nginx logs (maran-panel), the customers' site logs (maran-sites) and the FTPS
# transfer log (maran-ftps); install.sh appends a full transcript to this file on every install,
# upgrade and re-run, and nothing ever removed a line of it. It grows slowly — one transcript per
# run — which is exactly why it was missed: on a host that is upgraded for years it is the one file
# in the tree that only ever gets longer.
#
# The panel's and the agent's own logs are not files: both write to standard output, which systemd
# puts in the journal, and the journal is bounded by journald's own limits (SystemMaxUse=, by
# default a tenth of the filesystem it lives on, capped at 4 GiB). /var/log/maran/panel exists for
# a file sink an operator might configure, and no shipped build writes one — so it has no stanza
# here, and a log that starts being written there gets a stanza of its own in the same change.
#
# RETENTION: monthly, twelve months, compressed. A transcript is read when an upgrade is being
# diagnosed, often long after it ran, and a year of them is a few hundred kilobytes compressed.
#
# Every other choice is the one the sibling policies argue, for the same reasons:
#
#   * The path is a literal under /var/log/maran, which install.sh makes root-owned and 0750 and
#     re-asserts on every run (harden_log_directory). No glob: logrotate runs as root, and a pattern
#     is how a rotation policy ends up reaching a file it was not written for.
#   * `su root root` names the privileges explicitly instead of letting logrotate infer them from the
#     directory's owner, so a later change of ownership cannot make the rotation run as whoever now
#     owns the tree.
#   * `nocreate`, NOT `create`: nothing writes this file between runs, and the next run's `tee -a`
#     creates it. A rotation that created an empty file would put a file on disk that no process
#     owns the mode of.
#   * No postrotate and no `copytruncate`. The only writer is an installer run's `tee`, which holds
#     the file open for that run alone; a rotation that lands mid-run moves the open inode aside and
#     the rest of that transcript is still written into it, so no line is lost.
#   * `missingok` for a host whose transcript an operator deleted, and `notifempty` because an empty
#     transcript records nothing worth a numbered file.

/var/log/maran/install.log {
    monthly
    rotate 12
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    delaycompress
    nocreate
    su root root
}
